- How would you adapt for streaming input or very large strings with limited memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to adapt sorting techniques to handle duplicate elements efficiently and to analyze complexity trade-offs between quicksort variants and mergesort.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partition using Dutch national flag algorithm
- Average vs worst-case complexity with duplicate keys
- In-place sorting with tail recursion optimization
- Space complexity comparison between quicksort and mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi) with lt/gt pointers
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  const pivot = a[lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;

  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) swap(a, lt++, i++);
    else if (a[i] > pivot) swap(a, i, gt--);
    else i++;
  }

  // Tail recurse on smaller subarray for O(log n) space
  if (gt - lo < hi - lt) {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt + 1;
  } else {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
    hi = lt - 1;
  }

  if (lo < hi) threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi);
}
```

## Test Plan
- **All equal**: Verify no swaps and linear time
- **Already sorted**: Confirm correct partitioning and maintain sorted order
- **No duplicates**: Validate against standard quicksort behavior
- **Random with duplicates**: Compare against built-in sort for correctness
- **Large duplicate ratio**: Measure performance improvement vs standard quicksort

## Complexity Comparison
- **Three-way quicksort**: O(n log m) average, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space
- **Standard mergesort**: O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space

Optimal when m ≪ n (many duplicate keys).

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of prefix-based searching using a practical, lightweight data structure. It combines Trie fundamentals with per-node augmentation to enable fast prefix queries in real time—useful in autocomplete systems, IP routing, and spell checking.

## Key Concepts
- **Trie structure**: Each node contains 26 children (one per letter) and maintains a pass counter representing how many words pass through that node
- **insert operation**: O(m) time, where m is word length; creates nodes as needed and increments pass at each node visited
- **count_with_prefix operation**: O(k) time, where k is prefix length; traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass counter
- **Handling duplicates**: Pass counters naturally accumulate, so duplicate insertions simply increment the counter without special handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Array(26).fill(null);
    this.pass = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) node.next[idx] = new TrieNode();
      node.pass++;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    node.pass++;
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      const idx = char.charCodeAt(0) - 97;
      if (!node.next[idx]) return 0;
      node = node.next[idx];
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
}

// Example usage
const trie = new Trie();
['cat', 'car', 'cart', 'dog', 'dot'].forEach(w => trie.insert(w));
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('ca')); // 3
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('car')); // 1
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('d')); // 2
console.log(trie.countWithPrefix('do')); // 2
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of in-place merging, a practical skill when space is constrained.

## Key Concepts
- In-place merging with end pointers
- Time O(m+n), space O(1)
- Edge cases: empty arrays, duplicates, negative numbers

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeInPlace(A, m, B, n) {
  let i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
  while (j >= 0) {
    if (i >= 0 && A[i] > B[j]) {
      A[k--] = A[i--];
    } else {
      A[k--] = B[j--];
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with random arrays and verify sortedness?
- How would you adapt if A has no extra space and you can only rotate segments?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical streaming pattern: sliding window analytics with per-user deduplication per category, real-time top-K, and lazy expirations to avoid full scans.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with expirations
- Hash table for lastEvent per (user_id, category)
- Per-category max-heap with lazy deletions
- Global category-score heap for top-K queries

## Code Example
```javascript
class QuickCategoryGrouper {
  constructor(W){ this.W=W; this.last=new Map(); this.catSums=new Map(); this.catHeaps=new Map(); this.catTotal=new Map(); }
  addEvent(e){ const key=`${e.user_id}:${e.category}`; const prev=this.last.get(key); if(prev && prev.ts> e.ts-W){ this._applyDelta(e.category, e.value - prev.value); } else { this._applyDelta(e.category, e.value); }
    this.last.set(key,{value:e.value, ts:e.ts}); this._pushToHeap(e.category,{value:e.value, user:e.user_id, ts:e.ts}); }
  _applyDelta(cat, delta){ this.catTotal.set(cat,(this.catTotal.get(cat)||0)+delta); }
  _pushToHeap(cat, item){ if(!this.catHeaps.has(cat)) this.catHeaps.set(cat, []); this.catHeaps.get(cat).push(item); }
  topKCategories(K, currentTime){ // lazy pop to ensure validity
    const res=[]; for(const [cat, total] of this.catTotal){ res.push({cat, total}); }
    res.sort((a,b)=> b.total - a.total); return res.slice(0,K).map(r=>r.cat); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle skewed category distributions and ensure stability during microbursts?
- What are the trade-offs of using a single global heap vs per-category heaps for top-K queries?

